## The idea

The base is the classic pair: a **Dictionary** for O(1) lookup and a **LinkedList** for recency (front = most recent, back = least recent). Each later stage adds one thing on top. **TTL**: every entry stores the time it expires, and a read of an expired entry removes it ("lazy expiry"). **Eviction prefers dead entries**: before throwing out a live entry, look at the few oldest entries for one that has already expired. **Sweep**: walk a limited number of entries from the old end and remove the expired ones, so memory is reclaimed without one huge pause. **Thread safety**: one lock around every call — a read moves a node, so a read is a write. **Resize**: shrink by evicting until we fit.

## Step by step

1. LruCache: Dictionary<key, list node> and a LinkedList of entries. TryGet moves the node to the front. Put updates in place or, when full, removes the last node first.
2. LruTtlCache: entries also hold ExpiresAt = now + ttl. TryGet: if now ≥ ExpiresAt, remove and report a miss. Put on an existing key renews the TTL.
3. EvictOne: look at up to 4 entries from the back; remove the first expired one found. If none is expired, remove the very last (least recently used).
4. SweepExpired(budget): walk at most `budget` entries from the back, removing expired ones. Return how many were removed.
5. Resize(capacity): store the new capacity, then call EvictOne while Count > capacity.
6. SyncLruTtlCache: hold an LruTtlCache and wrap every method in lock(_lock). A ConcurrentDictionary alone is wrong here — the linked list would still be unprotected.

## Worked example

**Input:** capacity 2, clock at 0: Put(1, ttl 10), Put(2, ttl 1000), TryGet(1)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Put(1, ttl 10) | list [1]; 1 expires at 10 |
| Put(2, ttl 1000) | list [2, 1]; 2 expires at 1000 |
| TryGet(1) | alive → move to front → list [1, 2] |
| clock = 20, Put(3) | full → EvictOne checks from the back: 2 is alive, 1 is expired → remove 1 |
| after | list [3, 2] |
| TryGet(2) | hit — plain LRU would have evicted 2 and kept the dead entry 1 |

**Result:** the expired entry is evicted before a live one

## Cost

TryGet, Put and EvictOne are O(1) (EvictOne looks at most 4 entries). SweepExpired is O(budget). Resize is O(entries removed). Memory O(capacity). With one lock, every thread queues on it; sharding is the next step (see follow-ups).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// ---------------- Stage 1: plain LRU ----------------
public class LruCache<TKey, TValue> {
    private class Entry {
        public TKey Key;
        public TValue Value;
    }

    private readonly int _capacity;
    private readonly Dictionary<TKey, LinkedListNode<Entry>> _map = new Dictionary<TKey, LinkedListNode<Entry>>();
    private readonly LinkedList<Entry> _order = new LinkedList<Entry>();     // First = most recent

    public LruCache(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(capacity));
        _capacity = capacity;
    }

    public int Count {
        get { return _map.Count; }
    }

    public bool TryGet(TKey key, out TValue value) {
        LinkedListNode<Entry> node;
        if (!_map.TryGetValue(key, out node)) {
            value = default;
            return false;
        }
        _order.Remove(node);            // a read is a use: move to the front
        _order.AddFirst(node);
        value = node.Value.Value;
        return true;
    }

    public void Put(TKey key, TValue value) {
        LinkedListNode<Entry> node;
        if (_map.TryGetValue(key, out node)) {
            node.Value.Value = value;   // update, and count it as a use
            _order.Remove(node);
            _order.AddFirst(node);
            return;
        }
        if (_map.Count == _capacity) {
            LinkedListNode<Entry> victim = _order.Last;     // least recently used
            _order.RemoveLast();
            _map.Remove(victim.Value.Key);
        }
        var entry = new Entry();
        entry.Key = key;
        entry.Value = value;
        _map[key] = _order.AddFirst(entry);
    }
}

// ---------------- Stages 2, 4, 5: LRU where each entry has a TTL ----------------
public class LruTtlCache<TKey, TValue> {
    private class Entry {
        public TKey Key;
        public TValue Value;
        public long ExpiresAt;          // clock time when the entry dies
    }

    private int _capacity;
    private readonly IClock _clock;
    private readonly Dictionary<TKey, LinkedListNode<Entry>> _map = new Dictionary<TKey, LinkedListNode<Entry>>();
    private readonly LinkedList<Entry> _order = new LinkedList<Entry>();     // First = most recent

    public LruTtlCache(int capacity, IClock clock) {
        if (capacity <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(capacity));
        if (clock == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(clock));
        _capacity = capacity;
        _clock = clock;
    }

    public int Count {
        get { return _map.Count; }
    }

    public bool TryGet(TKey key, out TValue value) {
        value = default;
        LinkedListNode<Entry> node;
        if (!_map.TryGetValue(key, out node)) return false;

        if (_clock.NowMs >= node.Value.ExpiresAt) {     // expired: remove it now
            RemoveNode(node);
            return false;
        }
        _order.Remove(node);
        _order.AddFirst(node);
        value = node.Value.Value;
        return true;
    }

    public void Put(TKey key, TValue value, long ttlMs) {
        if (ttlMs <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(ttlMs));
        long expiresAt = _clock.NowMs + ttlMs;

        LinkedListNode<Entry> node;
        if (_map.TryGetValue(key, out node)) {
            node.Value.Value = value;
            node.Value.ExpiresAt = expiresAt;            // putting again renews the TTL
            _order.Remove(node);
            _order.AddFirst(node);
            return;
        }

        if (_map.Count >= _capacity) EvictOne();

        var entry = new Entry();
        entry.Key = key;
        entry.Value = value;
        entry.ExpiresAt = expiresAt;
        _map[key] = _order.AddFirst(entry);
    }

    // Walk at most `budget` entries from the old end and remove the expired ones.
    public int SweepExpired(int budget) {
        long now = _clock.NowMs;
        int removed = 0;
        LinkedListNode<Entry> node = _order.Last;
        while (node != null && budget > 0) {
            LinkedListNode<Entry> previous = node.Previous;  // save it before we unlink node
            if (now >= node.Value.ExpiresAt) {
                RemoveNode(node);
                removed++;
            }
            node = previous;
            budget--;
        }
        return removed;
    }

    public void Resize(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(capacity));
        _capacity = capacity;
        while (_map.Count > _capacity) EvictOne();
    }

    // Prefer an expired victim near the old end; otherwise the least recently used one.
    // Looking at only 4 entries keeps this O(1).
    private void EvictOne() {
        long now = _clock.NowMs;
        LinkedListNode<Entry> node = _order.Last;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4 && node != null; i++) {
            if (now >= node.Value.ExpiresAt) {
                RemoveNode(node);
                return;
            }
            node = node.Previous;
        }
        RemoveNode(_order.Last);
    }

    private void RemoveNode(LinkedListNode<Entry> node) {
        _order.Remove(node);
        _map.Remove(node.Value.Key);
    }
}

// ---------------- Stage 3: thread-safe ----------------
// One lock around everything. TryGet changes the list, so it needs the lock too.
public sealed class SyncLruTtlCache<TKey, TValue> {
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly LruTtlCache<TKey, TValue> _inner;

    public SyncLruTtlCache(int capacity, IClock clock) {
        _inner = new LruTtlCache<TKey, TValue>(capacity, clock);
    }

    public bool TryGet(TKey key, out TValue value) {
        lock (_lock) { return _inner.TryGet(key, out value); }
    }

    public void Put(TKey key, TValue value, long ttlMs) {
        lock (_lock) { _inner.Put(key, value, ttlMs); }
    }

    public int SweepExpired(int budget) {
        lock (_lock) { return _inner.SweepExpired(budget); }
    }

    public void Resize(int capacity) {
        lock (_lock) { _inner.Resize(capacity); }
    }

    public int Count {
        get { lock (_lock) { return _inner.Count; } }
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Capacity 0? Re-put of an existing key? Get on a key that expired exactly now?**

Reject 0 at construction; update in place + renew; now == expiry is expired

**Q: Complexity of Get, Put and the sweep?**

O(1), O(1), O(budget) — and why the sweep is bounded

**Q: Where is the bottleneck?**

Not the algorithm — the single lock

**Q: Two threads call Get on different keys at once. What shared state do they touch?**

Both move nodes in one list: map + list invariant → one critical section

**Q: Lock or ConcurrentDictionary?**

Lock — the invariant spans two structures

**Q: 100× traffic, one hot key.**

Shard by hash; hot key still one shard → front cache or approximate recency

**Q: Memory pressure.**

Byte-weighted capacity; Resize down

**Q: What do you measure and alert on?**

Hit ratio (relative drop), evictions by reason, lock wait, size

**Q: The source behind it goes down.**

Serve stale with a bound, circuit breaker, collapse concurrent misses

